Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary American drama, naturalistic faces, worn urban interiors, and grounded Midwest-to-Pacific-Northwest locations.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LATE AFTERNOON

A worn fifth-floor Milwaukee apartment holds OLIVIA OLSEN, an elderly brown-haired woman with failing eyes, in her brown chair beneath a faded afghan. Her younger son, lean NARRATOR, carries grocery bags inside.

(SOUND: elevator cables groan, grocery bags rustle, game-show applause leaks from the television)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK, already broad-shouldered, reddish-brown hair uncombed and a scar over his left eyebrow, steps before furious MRS. ENGEL. A broken kitchen window gapes behind her.

(SOUND: children’s kickball bounces, glass tinkles onto linoleum, Mrs. Engel’s window rattles)

NICK
I did it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mark Conner kicked that ball through her kitchen. Nick took it, then gave Ma the cash for the window.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Adult NICK, massive and pockmarked, in a dark sleeveless shirt and work boots, approaches a curl bench. KEVIN MCCLEARY immediately abandons the barbell.

(SOUND: iron plates clang, treadmills hum, a locker door slams)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a modern-day warrior. When Nick walked in, people moved out of the way.

NICK

Fifty for warm-ups.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

OLIVIA searches a kitchen counter where pill bottles sit in a careful row. NARRATOR watches as she stares through him, lost.

(SOUND: pill bottles click together, a wall clock ticks, television game-show music chirps)

OLIVIA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Your pills are right here, Ma.

OLIVIA
Oh. Right. I see them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was losing things. Then she started losing time.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK looms beside NARRATOR at a crowded bar, his scarred face flushed beneath harsh Packers-game light.

(SOUND: football announcer calls, pool balls crack, beer glasses scrape wood)

NICK
Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

NICK
Do something before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

NARRATOR
I just needed to get away for a while.

NICK
Then get home and watch Ma.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

NARRATOR walks beneath old hanging lamps and towering shelves. He signs a card application with hesitant concentration, then selects Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: pages turn, radiator hisses, distant footsteps cross old wood floors)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when all this was right here for free?

LIBRARIAN EVELYN
Your plastic card will come in the mail.

NARRATOR
I can take this home?

LIBRARIAN EVELYN
You can.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR arrives by bicycle. SIONNA MCCALLISTER, pale and slim with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a fish-symbol necklace, black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a backpack, trips into him.

(SOUND: bicycle chain clicks, traffic passes, the pizzeria doorbell jingles)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
That’s different. What does it mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me for an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

NARRATOR
Would you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Her eyes were the color of sky before the sun comes up.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

SIONNA sits at a small back table in a loose green hooded top, backpack at her feet. NARRATOR approaches with Wuthering Heights tucked under his arm.

(SOUND: espresso machine steams, cups clink, low student chatter fills the café)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s about a guy and girl who grow apart but are really in love.

SIONNA
The moors are like the people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

SIONNA
Heathcliff’s love never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR
A love that never changes will destroy you.

SIONNA
Sometimes we have to let go of things, and accept what happens without grasping onto them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR struggles on a purple mat among silent students. SIONNA moves easily through the poses nearby, her blue eyes warm when she sees him collapse into Savasana.

(SOUND: controlled breathing, floorboards creak, a soft chime fades)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

EXT. CITY PARK - DAY

NARRATOR and SIONNA walk beneath autumn trees. Teenagers laugh from a corner near their skateboards; NARRATOR stiffens, then lets SIONNA take his hand.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels rattle, leaves scrape pavement, distant traffic swells)

NARRATOR
I feel like punching that kid in the face.

SIONNA
Why let other people bother you like that?

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

NARRATOR
You’re really something.

SIONNA
You’re really something too.

NARRATOR and SIONNA kiss as the teenagers’ laughter fades behind them.

(MUSIC: gentle acoustic guitar rises beneath the wind)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

OLIVIA sits frightened, clutching her chest. NARRATOR kneels beside her while NICK enters in his brown suede jacket, booted feet heavy on the floor.

(SOUND: Olivia’s labored breath, television static, keys strike the entry basket)

OLIVIA
My heart feels like it’s going to jump out of my chest.

NARRATOR
I’m calling Dr. Carson.

NICK
Ma missed her appointment yesterday. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I forgot. I was with someone.

NICK
A girl is worth all this?

NARRATOR
I said I was sorry.

NICK slams out. OLIVIA flinches in her chair.

(SOUND: apartment door slams, picture frames rattle, Olivia’s breath catches)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

OLIVIA lies in a hospital bed, thin and confused. NARRATOR dozes in a chair as SIONNA arrives with a blue weekly pill organizer.

(SOUND: monitor beeps, hospital cart wheels roll past, fluorescent lights buzz)

SIONNA
We’ll put every pill where it belongs. Morning and night.

NARRATOR
I’m going to keep her alive.

SIONNA
You don’t have to do it alone.

NARRATOR falls asleep. His hand slips from OLIVIA’S blanket.

EXT. MEDIEVAL BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

NARRATOR, now in a mud-stained red tunic and leather boots, stands amid fallen soldiers. A younger, armored NICK rushes through smoke toward him.

(SOUND: swords clash, wounded men groan, wind whips battle flags)

NARRATOR
Nick! Thank God you’re here.

NICK
That you are, little brother.

NICK drives a long knife into NARRATOR’S chest.

(SOUND: blade tears cloth, NARRATOR gasps, battle noise abruptly drops away)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR bolts awake, drenched in sweat. OLIVIA sleeps on, unaware.

(SOUND: monitor beeps steadily, rain taps the window, NARRATOR’S breath trembles)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would Nick want to kill me?

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK confronts LINDSEY and her new boyfriend near the pool tables. NARRATOR steps between them as NICK’S fists clench.

(SOUND: hard rock music blares, pool balls stop rolling, bar chatter falls silent)

NARRATOR
You don’t even know this guy. Leave it, man.

NICK
Keep out of it.

NARRATOR
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK swings. NARRATOR reels backward as JOE and BOUNCER MARTIN seize NICK.

(SOUND: fist cracks against NARRATOR’S cheek, chairs scrape, Lindsey screams)

LINDSEY
Stop!

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

NARRATOR runs through cold streets and reaches SIONNA’S porch, blood at his lip. She pulls him inside.

(SOUND: NARRATOR’S ragged breathing, distant siren, rain begins on porch steps)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick.

SIONNA
No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I love him. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You can love him and still refuse to be ruled by him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK visits OLIVIA after weeks away. SIONNA serves peach pie while NARRATOR watches every movement between them.

(SOUND: forks scrape plates, television detective dialogue murmurs, ice cream spoon taps china)

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick.

NICK
Likewise.

NICK
I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
I study English and philosophy. I hope to teach.

NICK
Sometimes all that school doesn’t pay off.

SIONNA
You can’t keep a good woman down.

NICK’S smile thins. OLIVIA beams at SIONNA.

OLIVIA
She helps with my pills. Everything is straightening out nicely.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment stands silent. NARRATOR enters and finds OLIVIA’S chair empty. A red message light blinks on the telephone.

(SOUND: refrigerator hum, telephone message beep repeats, distant city traffic)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis. We need to speak with you regarding your family.

NARRATOR presses the telephone to his ear, his face draining.

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, gray-haired and solemn, sits across from NARRATOR in a stark interview room.

(SOUND: fluorescent lights buzz, typewriters clack beyond the door, a radiator knocks)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother and mother were struck at Werner and Lowe last night.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your mother passed away at the scene.

NARRATOR grips a paper cup until water spills over his hand.

NARRATOR
Nick killed her.

INT. SACRED HEART FUNERAL HOME - DAY

A maple casket rests beneath flowers. NARRATOR, in an ill-fitting dark suit, stands with SIONNA while FATHER JOSEPH closes a prayer book.

(SOUND: organ notes fade, muffled sobs, dirt patters softly onto the casket lid)

NARRATOR drops a handful of dirt onto the casket.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One day you’re talking to her. The next day you’re putting her in a box.

SIONNA holds NARRATOR’S shaking hand.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA, now wearing a shoulder brace after recovering from the stabbing, sits vacant in a rocking chair. NARRATOR kneels before her.

(SOUND: rocking chair creaks, rain runs down window glass, a kettle clicks off)

NARRATOR
You know I love you, right?

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.

SIONNA looks down. NARRATOR bows his head, unable to answer.

(MUSIC: a low, aching cello note holds beneath the rain)

EXT. PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Eight years later, Seattle rain glazes the brick streets. NARRATOR, older, steadier, in a dark wool coat, walks past market stalls toward his successful Tony’s Boy’s Pizza shop.

(SOUND: gulls cry, rain ticks on awnings, vendors call over market crowds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I became someone else. A man in Washington with twelve pizza places and a life that looked successful from the outside.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits cross-legged among candles, eyes closed. His old anger flickers across his face, then loosens with a slow exhale.

(SOUND: meditation bell rings, synchronized breathing, rain whispers against high windows)

INSTRUCTOR MAYA
What are you grateful for?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought of Ma. I thought of Sionna. I thought of Nick.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding resentment kept me in my own prison.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITING ROOM - DAY

Behind thick Plexiglas, NICK appears older and diminished: thinning reddish-brown hair graying at the temples, pockmarked face slack, prison uniform hanging on his once-powerful frame. NARRATOR lifts the phone.

(SOUND: locked doors buzz, telephones murmur, guards’ shoes echo on concrete)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

NARRATOR
I’m here to tell you I don’t want to see you again.

NICK lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR
You killed our mother. You held me down my whole life.

NARRATOR
And you tried to kill the love of my life.

NICK
You’re right.

NARRATOR
But I forgive you.

NICK looks up, stunned.

(SOUND: a guard’s radio crackles, a distant cell door slams)

NARRATOR
I forgive you because it sets me free.

NICK
I’m so sorry.

NICK
I am a huge asshole.

NARRATOR
I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What happened with you?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’re expanding.

NICK
President Olson.

NARRATOR
That’s me.

NICK cries openly as the guard approaches.

(SOUND: visitation timer buzzes, receiver clicks against Plexiglas)

NICK
I’m proud of you, man.

NARRATOR places the receiver down and watches NICK disappear through the locked door.

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DUSK

NARRATOR walks beside the gray water as a ferry crosses Puget Sound. He stops, breathing in the rain, then continues toward the city lights.

(SOUND: ferry horn calls across the water, waves slap pilings, gulls wheel overhead)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
